    "turn_text": "Well, if it's not the most, it's definitely a close second. And I don't think that's just my assessment. I think that's the president's view. He's quite proud. This was and why. One of the principal strategies of China is that the US-Japan-R-OK relationship can never get on the same page. That changed on August 18th at Camp David. And then China immediately said, well, we want our trilateral meeting. They just had a foreign minister's meeting of which their foreign minister refused to have a meal or a press conference at. So it fundamentally recalibrated the calculations of China. And it goes to our core strength. We have allies. China does not have allies. We have allies. All three countries in the last year wrote their new national security document. Unbelievably complementary. Unbelievably in sync with how they see the region. And the other thing is It aligns the countries politically, diplomatically, and from a defense side capacity. Since August 18, there have been 18 separate trilateral gatherings, either heads of state, secretary of state, weekend from now. I'll be traveling to Korea for the National Security Advisors trilateral meeting. They've had on the defense side, on the intelligence side. So it's an incredible kind of activity on this. Now look, here's my other thing if I could take one moment to make this point. I think you and I can both agree when you look at Europe, you look at the Middle East. Kind of dark. It's complicated. Too major wars. It's also, yeah. Look, the United States has a complicated relationship with Japan. We have a complicated relationship with Korea. Japan and Korea have a complicated relationship. What stood out in Camp David was we're diplomacy dialogue in the future, Trump the past kind of occupying and basically immobilizing leaders. And to President Biden's great credit, and I think this goes to not all politics is just local. It's also personal. He built up, I can say, right here in Japan, I can say to the Korea situation, an incredible relationship with Prime Minister Kushida, which allowed him not to do the bare minimum but to extend himself and to get to that place to Camp David. required leadership on all three leaders side, but a comfort capacity by the two leaders in confidence in both President Biden and in the United States. They took a step, look, you could argue both leaders, politics would be better if they didn't. But",
